/* Genetics, Genetics. Original cards. */
(window.PA_CARDS = window.PA_CARDS || {}).gen = (window.PA_CARDS.gen || []).concat([
/* ---------- Molecular Biology ---------- */
["Molecular Biology","Building blocks of DNA","What two parts make up a nucleoside?","A nitrogenous base and a pentose sugar","Add one or more phosphate groups to a nucleoside and it becomes a nucleotide, the true repeating unit of DNA and RNA."],
["Molecular Biology","Building blocks of DNA","Which DNA bases are purines?","Adenine and guanine","Purines have a double ring. Each purine pairs with a single-ringed pyrimidine, which keeps the width of the double helix constant."],
["Molecular Biology","Building blocks of DNA","Which base pairs form in double-stranded DNA?","Adenine with thymine, and guanine with cytosine","The shapes and hydrogen bonds only fit these pairings, so a pairing such as thymine with guanine does not occur in normal DNA."],
["Molecular Biology","Genome organisation","How many autosomes are in a normal human somatic cell?","44 (22 pairs)","Somatic cells have 46 chromosomes in total: 44 autosomes plus 2 sex chromosomes."],
["Molecular Biology","Genome organisation","Roughly what proportion of the human genome codes for protein?","About 1 to 2%","Most DNA is introns, regulatory regions and repeats. Exons that code for protein are a tiny fraction."],
["Molecular Biology","Transcription and RNA processing","What is transcription?","Making an RNA copy from a DNA template","It happens in the nucleus. Translation, which makes protein from mRNA, happens later in the cytoplasm."],
["Molecular Biology","Transcription and RNA processing","What does RNA polymerase do?","Builds RNA using DNA as the template","RNA polymerase II makes the messenger RNA for protein-coding genes."],
["Molecular Biology","Transcription and RNA processing","What does reverse transcriptase do?","Makes DNA from an RNA template","Retroviruses use it, and laboratories use it to make complementary DNA (cDNA) from mRNA."],
["Molecular Biology","Transcription and RNA processing","What is removed from newly made RNA during splicing?","Introns","The spliceosome cuts out non-coding introns and joins the exons to give mature mRNA."],
["Molecular Biology","Transcription and RNA processing","What process lets one gene produce several different proteins?","Alternative splicing","Exons are joined in different combinations, so one gene gives several mRNAs and proteins. It occurs in most human genes."],
["Molecular Biology","Transcription and RNA processing","What does small interfering RNA (siRNA) do in experiments?","Reduces (knocks down) expression of a target gene","siRNA pairs with the matching mRNA and triggers its destruction, so less protein is made."],
["Molecular Biology","Translation and the genetic code","How many possible codons are there?","64","There are 4 bases and 3 positions in a codon, so 4 x 4 x 4 = 64."],
["Molecular Biology","Translation and the genetic code","Why can one amino acid have several codons?","The code is degenerate (redundant): 61 sense codons code for only 20 amino acids","This redundancy is why some base changes are silent and do not change the protein."],
["Molecular Biology","Translation and the genetic code","Which codon starts translation, and which amino acid does it code for?","AUG, coding for methionine","Every new protein chain begins with methionine, though it is often removed later."],
["Molecular Biology","Translation and the genetic code","Name the three stop codons.","UAA, UAG and UGA","They code for no amino acid. Release factors bind and end the protein chain."],
["Molecular Biology","Translation and the genetic code","What molecule links an mRNA codon to the right amino acid?","Transfer RNA (tRNA)","Each tRNA has an anticodon that pairs with the codon and carries the matching amino acid."],
["Molecular Biology","Translation and the genetic code","Which enzyme loads the correct amino acid onto its tRNA?","Aminoacyl-tRNA synthetase","This loading step (aminoacylation) is where the code is really read, so its accuracy decides protein accuracy."],
["Molecular Biology","Translation and the genetic code","Name two post-translational modifications of proteins.","Phosphorylation and glycosylation","These change a protein after it is made. Phosphorylation often switches activity on or off; glycosylation adds sugar groups."],
["Molecular Biology","Translation and the genetic code","Which organelle modifies, packages and briefly stores newly made proteins?","The Golgi apparatus","Proteins from the rough endoplasmic reticulum go to the Golgi to be sorted into vesicles."],
["Molecular Biology","Cell cycle and cell division","In which cell-cycle phase is DNA copied?","S (synthesis) phase","Interphase is G1, S and G2 together: the cell grows and copies its DNA to get ready for division."],
["Molecular Biology","Cell cycle and cell division","In which cell-cycle phase do mature neurons stay?","G0","Neurons have left the cycle and do not divide. This is one reason the brain repairs poorly."],
["Molecular Biology","Cell cycle and cell division","In which stage of mitosis do chromosomes line up at the equator?","Metaphase","Spindle fibres line chromosomes up on the metaphase plate. Sister chromatids then pull apart in anaphase."],
["Molecular Biology","Cell cycle and cell division","How many cells does one round of meiosis make, and what is their ploidy?","Four haploid cells","Meiosis has two divisions. The first separates homologous pairs and the second separates sister chromatids."],
["Molecular Biology","Cell cycle and cell division","When does crossing over (recombination) happen?","Prophase of meiosis I","Paired homologues swap DNA, making new allele combinations. Linkage mapping depends on this."],
["Molecular Biology","Cell cycle and cell division","Why does the risk of nondisjunction rise with maternal age?","Oocytes stay paused in prophase I from fetal life until ovulation, sometimes for 40 or more years","Over time the links holding homologues together weaken, so chromosomes are more likely to separate wrongly."],
/* ---------- Mutations and Variation ---------- */
["Mutations and Variation","Types of mutation","What is a missense mutation?","A base change that makes a codon code for a different amino acid","The effect ranges from none to severe, depending on where the amino acid sits in the protein. Sickle cell disease is a classic example."],
["Mutations and Variation","Types of mutation","What is a nonsense mutation?","A base change that creates a stop codon","The protein is cut short and usually does not work, or the mRNA is destroyed early."],
["Mutations and Variation","Types of mutation","What is a silent mutation?","A base change that does not change the amino acid","Because the code is redundant, many third-position changes keep the same amino acid."],
["Mutations and Variation","Types of mutation","What is a frameshift mutation?","An insertion or deletion of bases not in multiples of three","Every codon after it is read in the wrong frame, so the protein is usually badly changed or cut short."],
["Mutations and Variation","Types of mutation","What is an in-frame mutation?","An insertion or deletion in multiples of three bases","Whole codons are added or lost, so the reading frame after it stays normal."],
["Mutations and Variation","Types of mutation","What is a transition mutation?","A purine replaced by a purine, or a pyrimidine by a pyrimidine (for example A to G)","Transitions are the commonest point mutations because the replacement base has the same ring structure."],
["Mutations and Variation","Types of mutation","What is a transversion mutation?","A purine swapped for a pyrimidine or the reverse (for example A to T)","The ring structure changes, so transversions are less common than transitions."],
["Mutations and Variation","Genetic variation","What is a polymorphism?","A common DNA variant, present in at least 1% of the population, without obvious disease effect","A variant this common is unlikely to be a new random mutation and is thought to be kept by evolutionary processes."],
["Mutations and Variation","Genetic variation","What is a single nucleotide polymorphism (SNP)?","A single base position where people commonly differ","SNPs are the commonest kind of variation and are the markers used in genome-wide association studies."],
["Mutations and Variation","Genetic variation","What is a copy number variant (CNV)?","A deletion or duplication of a stretch of DNA, usually over 1 kilobase","CNVs change gene dosage. Rare large CNVs carry high risk for schizophrenia, autism and intellectual disability."],
["Mutations and Variation","Genetic variation","What is a de novo mutation?","A new mutation present in the child but not in either parent's blood DNA","It arises in a parent's sperm or egg. De novo variants explain many cases of severe intellectual disability and autism."],
["Mutations and Variation","Genetic variation","Why does older paternal age raise the risk of de novo mutations?","Sperm precursor cells keep dividing throughout life, and each copy of DNA adds errors","Older paternal age is linked with a small rise in risk of autism and schizophrenia."],
/* ---------- Laboratory Techniques ---------- */
["Laboratory Techniques","Polymerase chain reaction","What does PCR do?","Makes millions of copies of a chosen DNA sequence","Primers mark the start and end of the target. Copies double with each cycle, so tiny samples become usable."],
["Laboratory Techniques","Polymerase chain reaction","What are the three steps of each PCR cycle, in order?","Denaturation by heat, annealing of primers, extension by DNA polymerase","Heat separates the strands, cooling lets primers bind, and a heat-stable polymerase builds new strands."],
["Laboratory Techniques","Polymerase chain reaction","Is gel electrophoresis part of the PCR reaction itself?","No","Electrophoresis is used afterwards to check the size of the product. PCR is just denature, anneal, extend, repeated."],
["Laboratory Techniques","Polymerase chain reaction","Are cloning vectors used in PCR?","No, vectors are used in DNA cloning","PCR copies DNA in a tube. Cloning puts DNA into a vector such as a plasmid and grows it in bacteria."],
["Laboratory Techniques","Blotting and cloning","Which blot detects DNA, which RNA and which protein?","Southern: DNA. Northern: RNA. Western: protein","Southern is named after Edwin Southern. The others are wordplay on his name."],
["Laboratory Techniques","Blotting and cloning","Which technique shows restriction fragment length polymorphisms (RFLPs)?","Southern blotting","DNA is cut with restriction enzymes, run on a gel, moved to a membrane and found with a labelled probe."],
["Laboratory Techniques","Blotting and cloning","What is a vector in DNA cloning?","A carrier, such as a plasmid, that holds a DNA fragment so it can be copied in bacteria","The vector provides the machinery for the insert to be stored and copied."],
["Laboratory Techniques","Cytogenetics and sequencing","What does a karyotype show?","The number and large structure of chromosomes","It shows aneuploidies such as trisomy 21 and large rearrangements, but misses small deletions below about 5 megabases."],
["Laboratory Techniques","Cytogenetics and sequencing","A 32-year-old man sees his GP about infertility. He is tall with small firm testes. Which test confirms the likely diagnosis?","Karyotype (chromosome analysis)","It shows the extra X chromosome (47,XXY) directly."],
["Laboratory Techniques","Cytogenetics and sequencing","What is fluorescence in situ hybridisation (FISH) used for?","Finding a known DNA sequence on chromosomes, such as a suspected microdeletion","A labelled probe sticks to its matching sequence. A missing signal shows a deletion, for example in 22q11.2 or Williams syndrome."],
["Laboratory Techniques","Cytogenetics and sequencing","What is the first-line genetic test for a child with unexplained intellectual disability or autism?","Chromosomal microarray","It finds copy number variants far smaller than a karyotype can see, and has a higher diagnostic yield."],
["Laboratory Techniques","Cytogenetics and sequencing","How is a suspected trinucleotide repeat expansion usually measured?","PCR-based repeat sizing (Southern blot for very large expansions)","PCR measures the repeat length quickly. Very large expansions, such as full fragile X mutations, may need Southern blot."],
["Laboratory Techniques","Animal and functional studies","What is a knockout mouse?","A mouse in which a chosen gene has been switched off","Watching what goes wrong without the gene shows what the gene normally does."],
["Laboratory Techniques","Animal and functional studies","What is a transgenic animal?","An animal carrying a gene inserted from elsewhere, often from another species","Transgenic models show how a gene works and can model human disease mutations."],
/* ---------- Inheritance and Population Genetics ---------- */
["Inheritance and Population Genetics","Mendelian principles","What is Mendel's law of segregation?","Each parent's two alleles separate in meiosis, so each gamete gets only one","This explains why a carrier passes the faulty allele to about half of their gametes."],
["Inheritance and Population Genetics","Mendelian principles","What is a genotype?","The set of genes (alleles) an individual carries","The phenotype is the observable result of the genotype acting with the environment."],
["Inheritance and Population Genetics","Mendelian principles","What is penetrance?","The proportion of people with a disease genotype who show the disease","In incomplete penetrance, some carriers are never affected. Huntington's alleles of 36 to 39 repeats show this."],
["Inheritance and Population Genetics","Mendelian principles","What is variable expressivity?","People with the same mutation show the disease with different severity","Neurofibromatosis type 1 is a classic example: family members range from a few cafe-au-lait spots to severe disease."],
["Inheritance and Population Genetics","Mendelian principles","What is pleiotropy?","One gene affecting several unrelated traits","Untreated phenylketonuria causes intellectual disability, fair skin and eczema from one enzyme defect."],
["Inheritance and Population Genetics","Mendelian principles","What is locus heterogeneity?","The same disease caused by mutations at different genes","Early-onset Alzheimer's disease can come from APP, PSEN1 or PSEN2. Testing one gene alone can miss the cause."],
["Inheritance and Population Genetics","Mendelian principles","What is epistasis?","An interaction in which one gene changes the effect of another gene","It can occur in the same biochemical pathway and makes simple prediction from single genes unreliable."],
["Inheritance and Population Genetics","Patterns of inheritance","What pedigree pattern suggests autosomal recessive inheritance?","Unaffected carrier parents, affected siblings, often skipping generations, both sexes equally","Carriers are well, so the disease may seem to appear from nowhere. Consanguinity raises the chance."],
["Inheritance and Population Genetics","Patterns of inheritance","Two carriers of the same autosomal recessive condition have a child. What are the chances?","1 in 4 affected, 1 in 2 carrier, 1 in 4 unaffected non-carrier","Each parent passes either allele with equal chance, giving these four equal combinations."],
["Inheritance and Population Genetics","Patterns of inheritance","A 30-year-old woman and her partner both have the same autosomal recessive disorder. What is the risk for each child they have?","100%","Both parents have two faulty copies, so every child must inherit two faulty copies."],
["Inheritance and Population Genetics","Patterns of inheritance","A 30-year-old woman with an autosomal recessive disorder has children with a non-carrier man. What will the children be?","All carriers, none affected","Each child gets one faulty allele from her and one normal allele from him."],
["Inheritance and Population Genetics","Patterns of inheritance","A 35-year-old man is heterozygous for an autosomal dominant disease and his partner is unaffected. What is each child's risk?","50%, whatever the child's sex","He passes the faulty allele to half his gametes, and autosomes are not linked to sex."],
["Inheritance and Population Genetics","Patterns of inheritance","Which pedigree feature rules out X-linked inheritance?","Male-to-male transmission","A father gives his son a Y chromosome, not an X, so X-linked conditions cannot pass from father to son."],
["Inheritance and Population Genetics","Patterns of inheritance","A 35-year-old man has an X-linked recessive disorder. What happens to his children (partner not a carrier)?","All daughters are carriers; no sons are affected","Daughters get his only X. Sons get his Y."],
["Inheritance and Population Genetics","Patterns of inheritance","A 25-year-old woman whose brother has an X-linked recessive disorder has mild signs of it herself. What is the likely explanation?","Skewed (unfavourable) X-inactivation (lyonisation)","If the X carrying the normal allele is switched off in most cells, the faulty allele is expressed. These women are 'manifesting heterozygotes'."],
["Inheritance and Population Genetics","Patterns of inheritance","What is the key feature of mitochondrial inheritance?","Passed from mothers to all their children, never from fathers","Only the egg gives mitochondria. Severity varies because cells hold different mixes of normal and faulty mtDNA (heteroplasmy)."],
["Inheritance and Population Genetics","Patterns of inheritance","What proportion of genes do half-siblings share on average?","25%","They share one parent, so they are second-degree relatives. Full siblings share 50% and first cousins 12.5%."],
["Inheritance and Population Genetics","Patterns of inheritance","A 30-year-old woman's father has Huntington's disease. What is her risk of inheriting the gene?","50%","Huntington's disease is autosomal dominant with near-full penetrance at 40 or more repeats, so each child of an affected parent has a 1 in 2 chance."],
["Inheritance and Population Genetics","Population genetics","State the Hardy-Weinberg equation.","p squared + 2pq + q squared = 1","p and q are allele frequencies. The terms give the frequencies of the two homozygotes and the heterozygote in a stable, randomly mating population."],
["Inheritance and Population Genetics","Population genetics","An autosomal recessive disease affects 1 in 10,000 people. Roughly how common are carriers?","About 1 in 50","q squared = 1/10,000, so q = 1/100. Carrier frequency is about 2q = 1 in 50."],
["Inheritance and Population Genetics","Population genetics","In 100 people, 36 are AA, 48 are Aa and 16 are aa. What is the frequency of allele A?","0.6","There are 200 alleles. A appears 72 + 48 = 120 times, and 120/200 = 0.6."],
["Inheritance and Population Genetics","Population genetics","What is assortative mating?","Choosing a partner who is similar to oneself in some trait","People with psychiatric disorders more often have partners with psychiatric disorders. This raises risk in offspring and can inflate heritability estimates."],
/* ---------- Chromosomal Disorders ---------- */
["Chromosomal Disorders","Chromosome number","What is aneuploidy?","An abnormal number of single chromosomes, for example trisomy or monosomy","Down syndrome (trisomy 21) is the commonest aneuploidy compatible with survival to adulthood."],
["Chromosomal Disorders","Chromosome number","A 31-year-old woman miscarries at 12 weeks. The fetus has 69 chromosomes in every cell. What is this called?","Triploidy","It means three full sets of chromosomes (3 x 23). Triploidy and tetraploidy almost always end in miscarriage."],
["Chromosomal Disorders","Chromosome number","What happens if nondisjunction of chromosome 21 occurs after fertilisation?","Mosaic trisomy 21","Only cells descended from the faulty division are trisomic, giving a mix of normal and trisomic cell lines."],
["Chromosomal Disorders","Chromosome number","A 12-year-old girl with mild learning difficulties has a 47,XXX karyotype. How many Barr bodies are in her cells?","Two","All X chromosomes except one are inactivated, so Barr bodies = number of X chromosomes minus one."],
["Chromosomal Disorders","Down syndrome","What is the commonest cause of Down syndrome?","Meiotic nondisjunction giving full trisomy 21 (about 95%)","It is mostly maternal in origin, which is why risk rises with maternal age."],
["Chromosomal Disorders","Down syndrome","A 29-year-old woman has a second baby with Down syndrome. She is well, but her own karyotype has 45 chromosomes. What is the likely mechanism?","Robertsonian translocation, usually t(14;21)","The carrier parent is well with 45 chromosomes. The child gets extra 21 material, so recurrence risk is higher than in simple trisomy."],
["Chromosomal Disorders","Down syndrome","Where do the chromosomes break and fuse in a Robertsonian translocation?","At or near the centromere of two acrocentric chromosomes","The two long arms join. The tiny short arms are lost without harm."],
["Chromosomal Disorders","Down syndrome","Which form of Down syndrome usually has the mildest cognitive impairment?","Mosaicism","Some cells are normal, so physical features are often milder and IQ may be higher."],
["Chromosomal Disorders","Down syndrome","Why do adults with Down syndrome develop Alzheimer's disease early?","They have three copies of the APP gene on chromosome 21","Extra amyloid precursor protein leads to amyloid plaques by about age 40, and dementia is common from the 50s."],
["Chromosomal Disorders","Down syndrome","What is the commonest heart defect in Down syndrome?","Atrioventricular septal (endocardial cushion) defect","About half of babies with Down syndrome have a heart defect, so all have an echocardiogram after birth."],
["Chromosomal Disorders","Down syndrome","A 42-year-old man with Down syndrome is referred to the learning disability team with tiredness, slowing and weight gain. Which blood test is essential?","Thyroid function tests","Hypothyroidism is common in Down syndrome and is a reversible cause of cognitive and functional decline that can mimic dementia."],
["Chromosomal Disorders","Other autosomal trisomies","Which trisomy causes rocker-bottom feet, clenched hands with overlapping fingers and a prominent occiput?","Trisomy 18 (Edwards syndrome)","It is the second commonest autosomal trisomy. Most affected babies die in the first year."],
["Chromosomal Disorders","Other autosomal trisomies","Which trisomy causes cleft lip and palate, polydactyly, small eyes and holoprosencephaly?","Trisomy 13 (Patau syndrome)","The midline brain and face fail to form properly. Survival beyond infancy is rare."],
["Chromosomal Disorders","Sex chromosome disorders","What are the typical features of Klinefelter syndrome?","Tall stature, small firm testes, infertility, gynaecomastia and low testosterone","Testicular failure raises gonadotrophins (hypergonadotrophic hypogonadism). Verbal IQ is often slightly lower and language problems are common."],
["Chromosomal Disorders","Sex chromosome disorders","A 19-year-old tall man with small testes and gynaecomastia has a buccal smear. What is seen?","One Barr body","He has two X chromosomes (47,XXY), and one is inactivated, so a Barr body is seen despite the male phenotype."],
["Chromosomal Disorders","Sex chromosome disorders","What is the karyotype of Turner syndrome?","45,X","Only one X chromosome is present. It is the only monosomy compatible with life."],
["Chromosomal Disorders","Sex chromosome disorders","What physical features are typical of Turner syndrome?","Short stature, webbed neck, low hairline, broad chest and ovarian failure","Coarctation of the aorta and horseshoe kidney are also associated. Growth hormone and oestrogen are used in treatment."],
["Chromosomal Disorders","Sex chromosome disorders","A 14-year-old girl with Turner syndrome is struggling with maths at school. How is her overall IQ likely to compare with her peers?","Usually normal","Specific weaknesses in visuospatial skills, maths and social cognition are common, but general intellectual disability is rare."],
["Chromosomal Disorders","Sex chromosome disorders","In Turner syndrome, which parent does the single remaining X usually come from?","The mother","The error usually involves loss of the paternal sex chromosome, so the remaining X is usually maternal."],
["Chromosomal Disorders","Sex chromosome disorders","A 16-year-old boy is very tall, has mild learning difficulties and behaviour problems at school. Which sex chromosome disorder fits?","47,XYY","XYY males are tall, may have slightly lower IQ and more ADHD, autism and behaviour problems. Most are never diagnosed."],
/* ---------- Behavioural Phenotypes ---------- */
["Behavioural Phenotypes","Microdeletion syndromes","Which chromosome region is deleted in velocardiofacial (DiGeorge) syndrome?","22q11.2","It is the commonest microdeletion syndrome, at about 1 in 4,000 births. Most deletions arise de novo."],
["Behavioural Phenotypes","Microdeletion syndromes","What are the physical features of 22q11.2 deletion syndrome?","Heart defects, cleft palate or velopharyngeal insufficiency, thymic hypoplasia, hypocalcaemia and typical facies","Remember 'CATCH-22'. Low calcium comes from parathyroid hypoplasia, so hypercalcaemia does not fit."],
["Behavioural Phenotypes","Microdeletion syndromes","What is the lifetime risk of psychosis in 22q11.2 deletion syndrome?","About 25%","It is the strongest single known genetic risk factor for schizophrenia. The deleted region includes COMT."],
["Behavioural Phenotypes","Microdeletion syndromes","Which deletion causes Williams syndrome?","7q11.23, including the elastin gene","Loss of elastin explains the supravalvular aortic stenosis and other vascular problems."],
["Behavioural Phenotypes","Microdeletion syndromes","What is the behavioural phenotype of Williams syndrome?","Over-friendly, socially disinhibited, with anxiety and relatively good verbal skills but poor visuospatial skills","Specific phobias, hyperacusis and ADHD are common. Over-friendliness with strangers makes them vulnerable."],
["Behavioural Phenotypes","Microdeletion syndromes","A 2-year-old girl in a paediatric clinic has an elfin face, supravalvular aortic stenosis and had high calcium as a baby. What is the diagnosis?","Williams syndrome","Elfin facies, supravalvular aortic stenosis and infantile hypercalcaemia form its classic triad."],
["Behavioural Phenotypes","Microdeletion syndromes","Which deletion causes cri du chat syndrome?","5p (short arm of chromosome 5)","Babies have a high-pitched, cat-like cry from larynx abnormalities, with microcephaly and marked intellectual disability."],
["Behavioural Phenotypes","Microdeletion syndromes","Which deletion causes Smith-Magenis syndrome?","17p11.2 (including the RAI1 gene)","Features include self-hugging, nail pulling, putting objects into body openings and severe sleep problems."],
["Behavioural Phenotypes","Microdeletion syndromes","Which gene is lost in Phelan-McDermid (22q13.3 deletion) syndrome, and what is the main psychiatric feature?","SHANK3; autism","SHANK3 builds the scaffold of glutamate synapses. Loss causes autism, absent or delayed speech and hypotonia."],
["Behavioural Phenotypes","Single-gene syndromes","What is the inheritance and gene in Lesch-Nyhan syndrome?","X-linked recessive; HPRT1 gene on Xq26","Absent HPRT enzyme leads to purine overproduction and very high uric acid. It affects boys."],
["Behavioural Phenotypes","Single-gene syndromes","A 4-year-old boy with developmental delay, dystonia and high uric acid bites his lips and fingers. What is the diagnosis?","Lesch-Nyhan syndrome","Compulsive self-injury, especially biting, is the hallmark. Allopurinol lowers uric acid but does not help the behaviour."],
["Behavioural Phenotypes","Single-gene syndromes","Which gene causes Rett syndrome, and how is it inherited?","MECP2 on Xq28; X-linked dominant, almost always de novo","It affects mainly girls. Affected males usually die early or have a different severe picture."],
["Behavioural Phenotypes","Single-gene syndromes","An 18-month-old girl seen in CAMHS lost her words and hand use after normal early development, and now wrings her hands. Her head growth has slowed. What is the likely diagnosis?","Rett syndrome","Regression after 6 to 18 months, midline hand stereotypies and slowed head growth are typical. Breathing problems and seizures often follow."],
["Behavioural Phenotypes","Single-gene syndromes","What genes cause tuberous sclerosis, and how is it inherited?","TSC1 (hamartin) and TSC2 (tuberin); autosomal dominant","About two thirds of cases are de novo. Loss of these tumour suppressors leads to hamartomas in many organs."],
["Behavioural Phenotypes","Single-gene syndromes","What proportion of people with tuberous sclerosis have autism?","About 40 to 50%","Autism risk is highest with infantile spasms and many cortical tubers. Intellectual disability affects about half."],
["Behavioural Phenotypes","Single-gene syndromes","Which gene causes neurofibromatosis type 1, and what does its protein do?","NF1 on 17q11.2; neurofibromin, a tumour suppressor","Autosomal dominant, with about half of cases de novo. Learning difficulties and ADHD are common."],
["Behavioural Phenotypes","Single-gene syndromes","What enzyme is deficient in classical phenylketonuria?","Phenylalanine hydroxylase","Phenylalanine builds up and harms the developing brain. It is autosomal recessive and found on newborn heel-prick screening."],
["Behavioural Phenotypes","Single-gene syndromes","A 35-year-old man with mild learning disability, fair skin, eczema and a musty smell was born abroad without newborn screening. Which enzyme is likely deficient?","Phenylalanine hydroxylase (phenylketonuria)","Untreated PKU gives intellectual disability and low melanin. A low-phenylalanine diet from birth prevents this."],
["Behavioural Phenotypes","Single-gene syndromes","How is Noonan syndrome inherited?","Autosomal dominant (often PTPN11)","Short stature, webbed neck, pulmonary stenosis and variable learning difficulty occur in both sexes."],
["Behavioural Phenotypes","Single-gene syndromes","How is Wilson's disease inherited, and which gene is involved?","Autosomal recessive; ATP7B on chromosome 13","Faulty copper transport causes liver disease, movement disorder and psychiatric symptoms. Caeruloplasmin is usually low."],
["Behavioural Phenotypes","Single-gene syndromes","A 19-year-old man on an acute ward has new psychosis, a tremor and abnormal liver tests. A brown ring is seen at the edge of his cornea. What is the likely diagnosis?","Wilson's disease","Kayser-Fleischer rings are copper deposits. Psychiatric symptoms can be the first sign, so check caeruloplasmin and urinary copper in young people with neuropsychiatric illness and liver signs."],
["Behavioural Phenotypes","Single-gene syndromes","A 17-year-old boy has not started puberty and has no sense of smell. His gonadotrophins are low. Which syndrome is likely?","Kallmann syndrome","GnRH neurons and smell neurons share a migration path from the nose, so one defect affects both. The classic X-linked form involves ANOS1."],
/* ---------- Repeats and Imprinting ---------- */
["Repeats and Imprinting","Trinucleotide repeat disorders","What is anticipation?","Earlier onset and greater severity in each new generation","Unstable repeats tend to grow when passed on, and longer repeats cause earlier, more severe illness."],
["Repeats and Imprinting","Trinucleotide repeat disorders","Which repeat and chromosome are involved in Huntington's disease?","CAG repeat in the HTT gene on 4p16.3","CAG codes for glutamine, so the huntingtin protein carries a long polyglutamine tract that forms nuclear inclusions."],
["Repeats and Imprinting","Trinucleotide repeat disorders","What CAG repeat lengths matter in Huntington's disease?","26 or fewer normal; 27 to 35 intermediate; 36 to 39 reduced penetrance; 40 or more full penetrance","Intermediate alleles do not cause disease but can expand into the disease range in children."],
["Repeats and Imprinting","Trinucleotide repeat disorders","In Huntington's disease, which parent's transmission causes most anticipation?","The father","The CAG repeat is more unstable in sperm production, so juvenile cases usually inherit the gene from their father."],
["Repeats and Imprinting","Trinucleotide repeat disorders","What is the link between CAG repeat length and age of onset in Huntington's disease?","Longer repeats give earlier onset","Juvenile (Westphal) Huntington's usually has more than 60 repeats and presents with rigidity rather than chorea."],
["Repeats and Imprinting","Trinucleotide repeat disorders","A 45-year-old woman in a memory clinic has chorea, irritability and cognitive decline. Her mother and grandmother had similar illness, starting younger each generation. What is the diagnosis?","Huntington's disease","Autosomal dominant inheritance, mid-life onset, chorea, psychiatric change and anticipation all fit."],
["Repeats and Imprinting","Trinucleotide repeat disorders","Which repeat and gene cause fragile X syndrome?","CGG repeat in FMR1 on Xq27.3","A full expansion silences the gene, so the FMRP protein, which controls synaptic protein synthesis, is not made."],
["Repeats and Imprinting","Trinucleotide repeat disorders","What CGG repeat ranges define fragile X status?","Under 45 normal; 45 to 54 intermediate; 55 to 200 premutation; over 200 full mutation","A full mutation becomes methylated and switches FMR1 off."],
["Repeats and Imprinting","Trinucleotide repeat disorders","Through which parent does a fragile X premutation expand to a full mutation?","The mother","Premutations expand in female meiosis. Fathers pass a premutation to all daughters, but it does not grow to a full mutation."],
["Repeats and Imprinting","Trinucleotide repeat disorders","What is the commonest inherited cause of intellectual disability?","Fragile X syndrome","Down syndrome is commoner but usually not inherited. Fragile X is also the commonest single-gene cause of autism."],
["Repeats and Imprinting","Trinucleotide repeat disorders","A 15-year-old boy has learning disability, a long face, large ears, large testes, gaze avoidance and social anxiety. What repeat is expanded?","CGG (fragile X syndrome)","Macroorchidism appears after puberty. Hand flapping, ADHD and hyperextensible joints are also common."],
["Repeats and Imprinting","Trinucleotide repeat disorders","What problems do fragile X premutation carriers face?","Men: tremor-ataxia syndrome (FXTAS) in later life. Women: premature ovarian insufficiency","Premutation carriers make toxic excess FMR1 RNA, unlike full mutations, where no protein is made."],
["Repeats and Imprinting","Trinucleotide repeat disorders","A 68-year-old man in a memory clinic has intention tremor, ataxia and memory decline. His grandson has fragile X syndrome. What is the likely diagnosis?","Fragile X tremor-ataxia syndrome (FXTAS)","He is probably a premutation carrier who passed it through his daughter, in whom it expanded. MRI may show middle cerebellar peduncle changes."],
["Repeats and Imprinting","Trinucleotide repeat disorders","Which repeat causes myotonic dystrophy type 1, and in which gene?","CTG repeat in DMPK on chromosome 19","It is autosomal dominant. Features include myotonia, cataracts, frontal balding, heart conduction defects and apathy."],
["Repeats and Imprinting","Genomic imprinting","What is genomic imprinting?","Expression of a gene depending on which parent it came from","The silenced copy is marked, mainly by DNA methylation, in the egg or sperm. Loss of the active copy then causes disease."],
["Repeats and Imprinting","Genomic imprinting","What is the commonest cause of Prader-Willi syndrome?","Deletion of paternal 15q11-q13 (about 70%)","Most of the rest have maternal uniparental disomy: two maternal copies and no paternal copy of chromosome 15."],
["Repeats and Imprinting","Genomic imprinting","What is the commonest cause of Angelman syndrome?","Deletion of maternal 15q11-q13 (about 70%)","The key gene is UBE3A, which neurons express only from the maternal copy. Paternal uniparental disomy and UBE3A mutations cause some cases."],
["Repeats and Imprinting","Genomic imprinting","What is the behavioural phenotype of Prader-Willi syndrome?","Hyperphagia and food seeking, skin picking, temper outbursts and obsessive-compulsive traits","Floppiness and poor feeding in infancy give way to overeating from early childhood. Obesity and short stature follow."],
["Repeats and Imprinting","Genomic imprinting","Which genetic subtype of Prader-Willi syndrome carries the highest risk of psychosis?","Maternal uniparental disomy","Affective psychosis is much more common in the disomy group than in the deletion group."],
["Repeats and Imprinting","Genomic imprinting","A 6-year-old boy with short stature, small hands and feet and obesity hoards food and picks his skin. What is the likely genetic cause?","Paternal deletion of 15q11-q13 (Prader-Willi syndrome)","Hyperphagia with short stature and skin picking is the classic picture. Hypogonadism is also usual."],
["Repeats and Imprinting","Genomic imprinting","A 4-year-old girl has severe learning disability, absent speech, seizures, an ataxic gait and frequent bursts of laughter. Her family history is clear. What is the mechanism?","Loss of the maternal 15q11-q13 contribution (Angelman syndrome)","Happy demeanour, ataxia and seizures are typical. Most cases are maternal deletions and are not inherited."],
/* ---------- Neurodegenerative Genetics ---------- */
["Neurodegenerative Genetics","Alzheimer's disease","Which three genes cause autosomal dominant early-onset Alzheimer's disease?","APP (chromosome 21), PSEN1 (chromosome 14) and PSEN2 (chromosome 1)","All raise production of the sticky amyloid-beta 42. PSEN1 is the commonest cause."],
["Neurodegenerative Genetics","Alzheimer's disease","A 48-year-old man in a memory clinic has two years of progressive memory loss. His father and aunt had dementia in their 40s. Which gene is most likely mutated?","PSEN1 (presenilin 1, chromosome 14)","Strongly familial dementia before 65 suggests a dominant gene. PSEN1 mutations are the commonest cause and can start as early as the 30s."],
["Neurodegenerative Genetics","Alzheimer's disease","Which gene is the main risk factor for late-onset Alzheimer's disease, and where is it?","APOE, on chromosome 19","APOE e4 raises risk and lowers age of onset. It is a risk gene, not a cause; many carriers never get dementia."],
["Neurodegenerative Genetics","Alzheimer's disease","How much does one APOE e4 allele raise Alzheimer's risk compared with no e4 allele?","About 3 times","Two copies raise risk about 8 to 15 times. APOE e2 is protective."],
["Neurodegenerative Genetics","Frontotemporal dementia","Which two FTD genes lie close together on chromosome 17q21?","MAPT (tau) and GRN (progranulin)","MAPT mutations give tau inclusions. GRN mutations give TDP-43 inclusions."],
["Neurodegenerative Genetics","Frontotemporal dementia","What FTD syndromes can progranulin (GRN) mutations cause?","Behavioural variant FTD, primary progressive aphasia and parkinsonism","GRN mutations lower progranulin, which is a growth factor for neurons. The pathology is TDP-43, not tau."],
["Neurodegenerative Genetics","Frontotemporal dementia","What is the commonest genetic cause of both FTD and motor neurone disease?","C9orf72 hexanucleotide (GGGGCC) repeat expansion","It is on chromosome 9. Carriers often present with psychosis or bizarre delusions."],
["Neurodegenerative Genetics","Vascular and prion disease","Which gene causes CADASIL, and how is it inherited?","NOTCH3 on chromosome 19; autosomal dominant","Granular material builds up in small artery walls, causing migraine with aura, mood disorder, strokes and dementia in mid-life."],
["Neurodegenerative Genetics","Vascular and prion disease","A 46-year-old woman with migraine with aura and depression has strokes. Her MRI shows white matter changes in the anterior temporal poles. Which gene should be tested?","NOTCH3 (CADASIL)","Anterior temporal and external capsule white matter lesions are typical. Skin biopsy may show granular osmiophilic material."],
["Neurodegenerative Genetics","Vascular and prion disease","Which gene encodes the prion protein, and which variant affects susceptibility?","PRNP on chromosome 20; codon 129 genotype","Methionine homozygosity at codon 129 raises susceptibility to sporadic and variant CJD."],
["Neurodegenerative Genetics","Parkinson's disease","Name three genes linked to familial Parkinson's disease.","SNCA (alpha-synuclein), LRRK2 and parkin (PRKN); also PINK1","LRRK2 is the commonest dominant cause. Parkin and PINK1 are recessive and cause young-onset disease."],
["Neurodegenerative Genetics","Parkinson's disease","Which genetic risk factor for Parkinson's disease is also linked to Gaucher disease?","GBA (glucocerebrosidase) mutations","Carrying one faulty GBA copy raises Parkinson's and Lewy body dementia risk. It is the commonest genetic risk factor for Parkinson's."],
/* ---------- Psychiatric Genetics ---------- */
["Psychiatric Genetics","Heritability and study designs","What does heritability measure?","The proportion of variation in a trait across a population that is due to genetic differences","It applies to a population in a given environment, not to an individual. It can change if the environment changes."],
["Psychiatric Genetics","Heritability and study designs","A disorder has a heritability of 75%. What does this mean?","75% of the variance in liability to the disorder in that population is due to genetic variation","It does not mean that an individual's illness is 75% genetic, or that 75% of relatives will be affected."],
["Psychiatric Genetics","Heritability and study designs","What does a family study show?","Whether a disorder runs in families (is familial)","Families share both genes and environment, so a family study cannot separate the two."],
["Psychiatric Genetics","Heritability and study designs","Which two study designs separate genetic from environmental effects?","Twin studies and adoption studies","Twins compare different genetic sharing in a shared environment. Adoption separates biological from rearing parents."],
["Psychiatric Genetics","Heritability and study designs","What is Falconer's formula?","Heritability = 2 x (MZ correlation minus DZ correlation)","Doubling the gap estimates the genetic part, because MZ pairs share twice the genetic similarity of DZ pairs."],
["Psychiatric Genetics","Heritability and study designs","What is the 'equal environments' assumption in twin studies?","MZ and DZ twins share their environments to the same degree","If MZ twins are treated more alike, heritability is overestimated. MZ twins also more often share a chorion in the womb."],
["Psychiatric Genetics","Heritability and study designs","Twins look alike in hair colour but differ in eye colour. What can be said?","They are concordant for hair colour and discordant for eye colour; they are probably dizygotic","Eye colour is almost wholly genetic, so a difference argues against identical twins. Looks alone cannot prove zygosity; DNA testing can."],
["Psychiatric Genetics","Linkage and association","What is linkage analysis?","Tracing whether a marker and a disease are passed on together within families","Close loci are rarely separated by recombination. It finds the rough location of genes of large effect."],
["Psychiatric Genetics","Linkage and association","What LOD score is usually taken as evidence of linkage?","3 or more","A LOD of 3 means odds of 1,000 to 1 in favour of linkage. A score of minus 2 or less is taken to exclude linkage."],
["Psychiatric Genetics","Linkage and association","What is a centimorgan?","The genetic distance giving a 1% chance of recombination between two loci","In humans this is roughly 1 million base pairs on average."],
["Psychiatric Genetics","Linkage and association","What is a whole genome scan in linkage studies?","Typing evenly spaced markers across the whole genome in families","It needs no prior idea of which gene matters. Neuregulin 1 was found this way in Icelandic families with schizophrenia."],
["Psychiatric Genetics","Linkage and association","What is gene mapping?","Any method that finds the chromosomal location of a gene","Linkage, sib-pair and association studies map genes. Family, twin and adoption studies only show whether genes matter."],
["Psychiatric Genetics","Linkage and association","What is an association study?","A comparison of allele frequencies between cases and controls","It detects common variants of small effect. Family-based designs use untransmitted parental alleles as internal controls."],
["Psychiatric Genetics","Linkage and association","What markers are used in a genome-wide association study (GWAS)?","Hundreds of thousands to millions of SNPs","GWAS relies on linkage disequilibrium: a typed SNP stands in for nearby causal variants."],
["Psychiatric Genetics","Linkage and association","What p-value threshold is used for genome-wide significance?","5 x 10 to the power minus 8","It corrects for about a million independent tests across the genome."],
["Psychiatric Genetics","Linkage and association","Which genetic model fits most psychiatric disorders?","Many common variants, each of small effect (polygenic), plus some rare variants of larger effect","Schizophrenia GWAS have found hundreds of loci, each changing risk only slightly."],
["Psychiatric Genetics","Linkage and association","What is a polygenic risk score?","A weighted sum of a person's many risk alleles found by GWAS","For schizophrenia it explains only a small part of variance in liability (under 10%), so it is not yet a clinical test."],
["Psychiatric Genetics","Linkage and association","What is an endophenotype?","A measurable, heritable trait between genes and illness that is present regardless of clinical state","It should co-segregate with illness and be commoner in unaffected relatives. Examples include smooth pursuit eye movement and P50 gating deficits."],
["Psychiatric Genetics","Schizophrenia","A 24-year-old woman's older brother has schizophrenia. What is her lifetime risk of schizophrenia?","About 9 to 10%","This is about ten times the general population risk of about 1%."],
["Psychiatric Genetics","Schizophrenia","A 28-year-old woman with schizophrenia asks at her CMHT review about the risk for a child with an unaffected partner. What is it?","About 13%","Risk to a child is similar to a sibling's. If both parents have schizophrenia, the risk rises to about 46%."],
["Psychiatric Genetics","Schizophrenia","What is the concordance for schizophrenia in monozygotic twins compared with dizygotic twins?","About 40 to 50% in MZ twins and about 10 to 15% in DZ twins","MZ concordance is well below 100%, so environment also matters."],
["Psychiatric Genetics","Schizophrenia","What happens to schizophrenia risk as relatives become more distant?","It falls sharply and non-linearly","Risk drops steeply from MZ co-twin to first-degree relatives, then to near population level. This fits many genes acting together."],
["Psychiatric Genetics","Schizophrenia","What is the heritability of schizophrenia?","About 80%","Twin studies give high estimates. SNP-based heritability is lower, which is part of the 'missing heritability' problem."],
["Psychiatric Genetics","Schizophrenia","Which large CNVs raise schizophrenia risk?","22q11.2 deletion, 3q29 deletion, 1q21.1 deletion, 15q13.3 deletion, 16p11.2 duplication and NRXN1 deletions","Each is rare but carries high risk. Most also raise risk of autism and intellectual disability."],
["Psychiatric Genetics","Schizophrenia","Name three candidate genes historically linked to schizophrenia.","Neuregulin 1, dysbindin (DTNBP1) and DISC1","DISC1 was found from a balanced translocation in a Scottish family. Candidate gene findings have mostly not held up in large GWAS."],
["Psychiatric Genetics","Schizophrenia","Which genome-wide hit for schizophrenia is a zinc finger gene on chromosome 2?","ZNF804A","It was one of the first GWAS-significant findings. Its exact role is still unclear."],
["Psychiatric Genetics","Schizophrenia","Which gene in the 22q11.2 region breaks down prefrontal dopamine?","COMT (catechol-O-methyltransferase)","The Val158Met variant changes enzyme activity: the Val allele breaks down dopamine faster, lowering prefrontal dopamine."],
["Psychiatric Genetics","Schizophrenia","Which X-linked enzyme gene is linked with aggression and antisocial behaviour rather than with schizophrenia?","Monoamine oxidase A (MAOA)","Low-activity MAOA variants, especially with childhood maltreatment, predict antisocial behaviour. A null mutation causes Brunner syndrome."],
["Psychiatric Genetics","Mood and other disorders","What is the heritability of bipolar disorder?","About 60 to 85%","It is among the most heritable psychiatric disorders, more so than IQ or personality traits."],
["Psychiatric Genetics","Mood and other disorders","A 22-year-old man's mother has bipolar disorder. How much is his risk of unipolar depression raised?","Increases it about 2 to 3 times","Relatives of people with bipolar disorder have higher risk of both bipolar and unipolar mood disorders."],
["Psychiatric Genetics","Mood and other disorders","What is the heritability of autism?","About 80%","Twin studies show high MZ concordance. Rare de novo variants and common variants both contribute."],
["Psychiatric Genetics","Mood and other disorders","A 34-year-old woman whose 3-year-old son was just diagnosed with autism asks about the risk for her next child. Roughly what is it?","About 10 to 20%","This is many times the population rate. Risk is higher if the sibling is a boy or two children are already affected."],
["Psychiatric Genetics","Mood and other disorders","A 17-year-old boy's mother and paternal aunt have alcohol dependence. Roughly how much is his risk raised?","About 3 to 4 times","Adoption studies show risk follows biological, not adoptive, parents. Heritability of alcohol dependence is about 50%."],
["Psychiatric Genetics","Mood and other disorders","On which chromosome is the serotonin transporter gene (SLC6A4)?","17q11.2","Its promoter variant, 5-HTTLPR, has short and long alleles. The short allele lowers transporter expression."],
/* ---------- Pharmacogenetics and Epigenetics ---------- */
["Pharmacogenetics and Epigenetics","Pharmacogenetics","What proportion of white Europeans are CYP2D6 poor metabolisers?","About 7%","They have two non-working alleles, so drugs cleared by CYP2D6 reach higher levels and cause more side effects."],
["Pharmacogenetics and Epigenetics","Pharmacogenetics","How does CYP2D6 ultrarapid metabolism arise, and what is its effect?","Extra copies of the gene; drugs are cleared faster and may not work","It is commoner in North African and Middle Eastern groups. Prodrugs such as codeine become toxic as more is turned into morphine."],
["Pharmacogenetics and Epigenetics","Pharmacogenetics","Name four psychotropics mainly metabolised by CYP2D6.","Aripiprazole, risperidone, haloperidol and atomoxetine (also many TCAs, fluoxetine and paroxetine)","In poor metabolisers, levels rise. The aripiprazole licence advises halving the dose in known CYP2D6 poor metabolisers."],
["Pharmacogenetics and Epigenetics","Pharmacogenetics","Which enzyme's genotype most affects citalopram and escitalopram levels?","CYP2C19","Poor metabolisers have higher levels and more risk of QTc prolongation. Poor metabolism is commoner in East Asian people."],
["Pharmacogenetics and Epigenetics","Pharmacogenetics","Which HLA allele predicts carbamazepine-induced Stevens-Johnson syndrome in Han Chinese and Thai people?","HLA-B*1502","The MHRA advises testing people of Han Chinese or Thai origin before starting carbamazepine, and avoiding it if positive."],
["Pharmacogenetics and Epigenetics","Pharmacogenetics","A 26-year-old woman of Han Chinese origin with bipolar disorder is being considered for carbamazepine. What test is needed first?","HLA-B*1502 genotyping","Carriers have a much higher risk of Stevens-Johnson syndrome and toxic epidermal necrolysis, which can be fatal."],
["Pharmacogenetics and Epigenetics","Pharmacogenetics","Which HLA allele is linked to carbamazepine hypersensitivity in Europeans and Japanese people?","HLA-A*3101","It is linked to a range of hypersensitivity reactions, including drug reaction with eosinophilia (DRESS)."],
["Pharmacogenetics and Epigenetics","Pharmacogenetics","Why is valproate contraindicated in POLG-related mitochondrial disease?","It can cause acute, often fatal, liver failure","POLG mutations impair mitochondrial DNA copying. Suspect them in unexplained seizures with developmental delay before starting valproate."],
["Pharmacogenetics and Epigenetics","Pharmacogenetics","A 52-year-old man on an acute ward has very high plasma levels and marked side effects on a standard dose of risperidone. What genetic explanation is most likely?","CYP2D6 poor metaboliser status","Poor metabolisers clear risperidone slowly. Check interacting drugs too, such as fluoxetine or paroxetine, which inhibit CYP2D6."],
["Pharmacogenetics and Epigenetics","Epigenetics","What is epigenetics?","Heritable changes in gene expression without changes in the DNA sequence","Marks on DNA and histones control which genes are switched on, and can be shaped by the environment."],
["Pharmacogenetics and Epigenetics","Epigenetics","Which two mechanisms explain most epigenetic regulation?","DNA methylation and histone modification","Non-coding RNAs, such as microRNAs, are a third mechanism."],
["Pharmacogenetics and Epigenetics","Epigenetics","What does methylation of a gene's promoter CpG island usually do?","Switches the gene off","Methyl groups on cytosine block transcription factors and attract proteins that close the chromatin."],
["Pharmacogenetics and Epigenetics","Epigenetics","What does histone acetylation do to gene expression?","Increases it","Acetylation loosens DNA around histones, so transcription machinery can reach the gene."],
["Pharmacogenetics and Epigenetics","Epigenetics","Which mood stabiliser is also a histone deacetylase inhibitor?","Valproate","By blocking HDACs it keeps chromatin open. This may contribute to its teratogenicity and is being studied in other conditions."],
["Pharmacogenetics and Epigenetics","Epigenetics","What protein does the Rett syndrome gene MECP2 make?","Methyl-CpG binding protein 2","It reads DNA methylation and regulates gene expression in neurons, so Rett syndrome is a disorder of epigenetic reading."],
["Pharmacogenetics and Epigenetics","Epigenetics","How does a full fragile X mutation stop FMR1 working?","Hypermethylation of the expanded repeat and promoter silences the gene","The DNA sequence of the coding region is normal, but the gene is switched off epigenetically."],
["Pharmacogenetics and Epigenetics","Epigenetics","What did rat studies of maternal licking and grooming show?","High maternal care lowered methylation of the hippocampal glucocorticoid receptor gene, giving calmer stress responses","Early care changed gene expression in the offspring for life. Cross-fostering showed it was the care, not genes."],
["Pharmacogenetics and Epigenetics","Epigenetics","What was found in people exposed before birth to the Dutch Hunger Winter famine?","About double the risk of schizophrenia, and lasting methylation changes such as at IGF2","Prenatal famine in early pregnancy left epigenetic marks still present 60 years later."]
]);
